<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ara Gaya</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 560 CE (approx.) · capital Haman</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A major Gaya kingdom at Haman, known for its flame-pattern stoneware and as host of diplomatic conferences between Baekje, Silla and Japan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ara Gaya, the Anra of Japanese sources, developed from Anya-guk, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a> statelet in the Haman basin southwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong</a>. According to Korean chronicles it was attacked in the early 3rd century by the eight port states of the coast and saved by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a> declined around 400, Ara Gaya became, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daegaya">Daegaya</a>, one of the two leading Gaya states. Hilltop tombs at Malisan hold armour, horse gear and distinctive stoneware with flame-shaped openings. In the 6th century it convened talks with envoys of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yamato">Yamato</a> as the Gaya states tried to survive between their neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Caught between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Baekje</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a>, Ara Gaya was taken by Silla around 560, shortly before the fall of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daegaya">Daegaya</a>, and became a Silla county.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ara-gaya.jpg" alt="Ara Gaya" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Trainholic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maisan(Mt)_in_Haman,_Gyeongsang-Namdo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 CE – 560 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Haman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaya language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aragaya, Alla, Anra</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=380&amp;focus=ara-gaya" aria-label="Ara Gaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7903 865 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ara Gaya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8247 1162h0M8260 1139h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8223 1121h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8223 1121h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Korea. <a href="/atlas?year=380&amp;focus=ara-gaya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

